Lake Constance in southern Germany the  Haitian restaurant and hotel overlooks  the western shore across from  Switzerland it's been a family-owned  establishment for seven generations  since 1821  for Euro Maxon the cart chef Marcos  Marek is preparing veal filet with  asparagus and nettle noodles he picks  the nettles in the hotel garden with  gloves on  that's because raw nettles sting if you  touch them stinging nettles grow around  the world but they're much more than  just weeds nettle tea is good against  bloating nettles promote healthy blood  as they have a lot of iron and a lot of  vitamins they're rich in vitamin C and a  and cooked they taste like spinach  besides nettles and veal Merrick needs  fresh asparagus for this dish as with  almost all his produce he gets it from  local farmers during the season which  lasts till late June German farmers  mostly harvest white asparagus but some  have other preferences bog for me real  asparagus is green which is still very  very popular in most of Europe even  though white asparagus is much more  popular in Germany it's just a lovely  tender vegetable in early spring from  hand to mouth almost in the kitchen  Marek first washes the nettles  thoroughly in warm water  flower for the pasta dough salt and eggs  they're all blended together with the  nettles into a dough then chef Marc  needs the dough by hand adding oil and  he lets it sit for an hour in the  refrigerator but there's always room for  experimentation if I can do nettle pasta  like this but I can also add beetroot of  different herbs and then I have a real  variety something you can't go out and  buy he adds butter and sugar to the  boiling water for the asparagus to give  it a sweet note and there's another  important detail the end pieces are  woody we don't want that but there's  also another reason this way the  asparagus is all the same length will  now cut them off like this  another tip don't throw away the end  pieces you can use them to make cream of  asparagus soup he bundles the asparagus  stalks into equal portions and then  boils them for six to eight minutes  it's time to take the dough out of the  refrigerator this should be sliced like  bread and then rolled out very thinly  with a pasta machine or a rolling pin  dust with flour and cut into strips then  boil in hot water pepper and salt the  veal and then fry on both sides in  butter then place it in the oven I  always put the meat in the oven between  100 and 110 degrees Celsius so that the  meat stays juicy I don't want the meat  to get tough or dry for the herb jacket  there's parsley and rosemary first Marik  fries the veal filet in time and bastes  it several times with hot butter then he  turns over the meat in the other herbs  the veal filet in an herb jacket with  asparagus stems and nettle pasta is now  ready and to go with it white wine and  of course this view of Lake Constance
 

